// =============================================================================
// mod.vr - Verum Standard Library Root
// =============================================================================
// Version: 0.1.0
// =============================================================================
//

// # Verum Standard Library
//

// The standard library follows a layered architecture where each layer
// depends only on lower layers:
//

// ```
// Layer 6: Cognitive (GPU, Tensors, Autodiff, Neural)
// Layer 5: Network (TCP, HTTP, TLS, WebSocket)
// Layer 4: Async (Future, Task, Channel, Select)
// Layer 3: I/O (File, Path, Read, Write, Buffer)
// Layer 2: Collections (List, Map, Set, Deque, Tree)
// Layer 1: Text (Text, Char, Format, Regex)
// Layer 0: Core (Primitives, Maybe, Result, Ordering, Iterator, Memory)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Kernel: V-LLSI Native (sys/, mem/) - Memory CBGR, TLS, Syscalls, I/O
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Layer M: Meta (Compile-time) - Code Generation, Reflection, Macros
// ```
//

// # V-LLSI Migration Status
//

// As of V-LLSI migration, the stdlib no longer depends on Rust FFI
// (verum_bootstrap, verum_runtime). All operations use native implementations:
//

// - Memory: Native CBGR allocator (mem/allocator.vr)
// - Panic: Native sys/init.vr panic handler
// - Context: Native TLS context system (sys/common.vr)
// - Async: Native completion-based I/O (io_uring/kqueue/IOCP)
// - Time: Native platform syscalls (sys/{linux,darwin,windows}/time.vr)
//

// Spec: docs/architecture/new-arch-spec.md
//

// # Usage
//

// ```verum
// mount core.base.*; // Core types and protocols
// mount core.collections.*; // List, Map, Set
// mount core.io.*; // File operations
// mount core.async.*; // Async/await support
// ```
//

// # Design Principles
//

// 1. **Semantic Honesty**: Types describe meaning (List, Text, Map),
//  not implementation (Vec, String, HashMap)
// 2. **No Magic**: All dependencies explicit via `mount`
// 3. **Gradual Safety**: Three-tier references (Tier0/1/2)
// 4. **Zero-Cost Abstractions**: CBGR enables memory safety at ~15ns
//

// =============================================================================

@arch_module(
    foundation: Foundation.ZfcTwoInacc,
    stratum: MsfsStratum.LFnd,
    lifecycle: Lifecycle.Definition
)
module core;

// =============================================================================
// V-LLSI Kernel Layer (sys/, mem/)
// =============================================================================

// Low-level system layer - direct syscalls, TLS, platform abstractions.
// Provides: OSError, FileDesc, IOVec, Context system, IOEngine protocol.
public module sys;

// Memory management layer - CBGR allocator, epoch management, hazard pointers.
// Provides: cbgr_alloc, AllocationHeader, ThinRef, FatRef, Epoch.
public module mem;

// Compiler intrinsics - low-level operations that map to CPU instructions.
// Provides: arithmetic, bitwise, float, memory, atomic, simd, platform.
// Architecture-specific: lowlevel/x86_64, lowlevel/aarch64.
public module intrinsics;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 0: Base (Depends on: sys, mem)
// =============================================================================

// Base types and protocols - the foundation of the standard library.
// Provides: Maybe, Result, Ordering, Heap, Shared, Iterator, protocols.
public module base;

// SIMD types and operations - portable vectorized computation.
// Provides: Vec<T,N>, Mask<N>, gather, scatter, reduce, shuffle.
public module simd;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 0: Byte primitives (Depends on: base)
// =============================================================================

// These four are computation over bytes, not security policy. They
// depend on `base` and nothing else, so any layer may use them —
// a Bloom filter needs a hash, not a dependency on the crypto stack.
// Each is split by the GUARANTEE it carries, because within each of
// them the variants are not interchangeable and a wrong pick fails
// silently. See the module headers for the choice rules.

// Hash functions: checksum / fast / crypto / legacy.
public module hash;

// Keyed authentication: HMAC, Poly1305.
public module mac;

// Randomness: secure (CSPRNG) vs deterministic (reproducible).
public module random;

// Operations defined by what must NOT happen: constant-time
// comparison, zeroization that survives optimisation.
public module subtle;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 2: Identifiers (Depends on: base, time, random)
// =============================================================================

// Unique identifiers: uuid, ulid, nanoid, snowflake. NOT byte
// primitives — they read a wall clock and draw entropy, which is why
// they are a layer above and not part of the group declared just here.
public module id;

// Currency-aware monetary value, on top of the V0 numeric quartet
// (Decimal / BigInt / BigDecimal / Rational): a `Money` type, an ISO
// 4217 `Currency` descriptor with ~25 predefined currencies, and
// minor-unit-exact arithmetic.
//

// Same omission `core.action` carried until it was wired below:
// `core/money/` declares `module core.money;` and its own submodules,
// so it resolved by exact path (`mount core.money.currency`) while
// being ABSENT from the stdlib root surface — nothing reachable from
// `core` could see it, and the prelude had nothing to re-export from.
public module money;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 0.5: Mathematics (Depends on: base, intrinsics, simd)
// =============================================================================

// Mathematical computing - from IEEE 754 to linear algebra.
// Layer 0: Foundation (IEEE 754, integers, bits, checked arithmetic)
// Layer 1: Scalar (elementary, hyperbolic, special functions)
// Layer 2: Linear (vectors, matrices, BLAS, decompositions)
// Layer 3: Analysis (differentiation, integration, ODEs, optimization)
// Provides: sin, cos, exp, log, sqrt, Matrix, Vector, solve, etc.
// Spec: docs/architecture/math-spec.md
public module math;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 1: Text (Depends on: core)
// =============================================================================

// Text processing: UTF-8 strings, formatting, character utilities.
public module text;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 2: Collections (Depends on: core, text)
// =============================================================================

// Data structures: List, Map, Set, Deque, BTree, PriorityQueue.
public module collections;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 2.5: Context (Depends on: base, collections, runtime)
// =============================================================================

// Context system: DI scope types, error types, provider utilities.
// Level 1 (@injectable/@inject): compile-time static DI, 0ns overhead.
// Level 2 (provide/using): runtime DI via task-local storage, ~5-30ns.
// Spec: docs/detailed/16-context-system.md
public module context;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 3: I/O (Depends on: core, text, collections)
// =============================================================================

// Input/output: Files, paths, streams, buffered I/O.
public module io;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 3.5: Time (Depends on: core)
// =============================================================================

// Time && duration: Duration, Instant, SystemTime.
public module time;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 3.5: Sync (Depends on: core, collections)
// =============================================================================

// Synchronization primitives: Mutex, RwLock, atomics, barriers.
// Note: Placed between I/O && Async as async depends on sync primitives.
public module sync;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4: Async (Depends on: core, collections, io, sync)
// =============================================================================

// Asynchronous programming: Futures, tasks, channels, executors.
public module async;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.5: Runtime (Depends on: core, collections, async, sync)
// =============================================================================

// Runtime system: supervisors, task spawning, execution environment, recovery.
public module runtime;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 3.3: Async signal subscription (Depends on: async, broadcast, sys/signal)
// =============================================================================

// Async wrappers for OS signals — ctrl_c, terminate, shutdown_signals,
// signal_stream. Backed by self-pipe-pattern over core.sys.signal flags.
// Spec: net-framework spec §7.8 (graceful shutdown)
public module signal;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 3.5: Terminal (Depends on: core, text, collections, io, sync, time)
// =============================================================================

// Terminal I/O and TUI framework: raw terminal, events, styles, rendering,
// layout engine, widget library, application framework (Elm Architecture).
// 7-layer architecture from raw termios to full TUI apps.
// Spec: docs/detailed/40-terminal-tui-architecture.md
public module term;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.7: Encoding (Depends on: core, text, collections)
// =============================================================================

// Data encoding and serialization: JSON (strict RFC 8259), base64, hex.
// Zero-allocation parsing where the ADT owns the decoded structure.
public module encoding;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.8: Security (Depends on: core, base, util)
// =============================================================================

// Crypto primitives: SHA-256, HMAC-SHA-256, HKDF-SHA-256, constant-time
// compare. Pure-Verum reference + pluggable HACL* / OpenSSL backend.
// Plus legacy labels.vr / regions.vr (capability/region models).
public module security;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.9: Compression (Depends on: core)
// =============================================================================

// Compression codecs: gzip / deflate / zlib (RFC 1950-1952), brotli
// (RFC 7932), zstd, lz4. HTTP Content-Encoding, WebSocket deflate, TLS
// cert-compression, QUIC/RDMA payload paths.
public module compress;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.91: Multi-purpose data stores (Depends on: core, net, sync, async)
// =============================================================================

// Redis (RESP3) — multi-purpose data store: cache, pub/sub,
// streams (event queue), transactions (MULTI/EXEC/WATCH), Lua
// scripting, sorted-set leaderboards, distributed locks. Layered
// design: protocol.vr / client.vr (low-level pool) + commands.vr
// (typed surface) + pubsub.vr / stream.vr / transaction.vr /
// script.vr.  Application-side adapters in core.cache /
// core.queue / etc. compose this for protocol-shaped surfaces.
public module redis;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.92: Caching (Depends on: redis)
// =============================================================================

// Abstract caching protocol — `CacheBackend` + concrete adapters in
// core.cache.adapters.*. Consumers compose against the abstract
// protocol; the concrete adapter is selected at construction.
public module cache;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.93: Search (Depends on: net.http)
// =============================================================================

// Abstract search-index protocol — `SearchIndex` + concrete
// adapters: MeiliSearch (v1 REST), future: Elasticsearch,
// Typesense, SQLite-FTS5. Faceted query algebra + paginated
// results + index configuration.
public module search;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.94: Object storage (Depends on: net.http, security.{hash, mac})
// =============================================================================

// Abstract object-store protocol — `ObjectStore` + concrete
// adapters: S3-compatible (AWS S3, MinIO, R2, Wasabi, Backblaze
// B2 S3 endpoint) with full SigV4 authentication, future: GCS,
// Azure Blob, local filesystem.
public module storage;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.9: Archive (Depends on: core, compress, encoding, security)
// =============================================================================

// Packaging subsystem mirroring core.compress: container formats
// (tar/USTAR/PAX, zip, ar, cpio) over the existing compression
// codecs. The Archive protocol + ArchiveAlgorithm enum compose
// against any underlying codec — `tar.zst`, `tar.gz`, `zip`-deflate,
// etc., picked at the Archive level.
public module archive;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.9.5: Configuration (Depends on: core, encoding)
// =============================================================================

// Universal configuration subsystem: ConfigValue ADT hub +
// ConfigFormat protocol + open registry. TOML / YAML / INI / JSON-as-
// config / TOML-Edit. Cross-format conversion with LossPolicy. Used
// by every cog manifest, every IDE settings file, every
// observability config.
public module configuration;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.9.6: Cog tooling (Depends on: core, base, security, archive,
//                                       configuration, encoding)
// =============================================================================

// First-class cog manipulation: typed CogManifest, .vbca archive
// reader, Ed25519 cog-signature envelope, pubgrub-style dependency
// resolver. Composed by the registry, by `verum publish/install`,
// and by IDE / LSP tooling.
public module cog;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.7.5: Protobuf (Depends on: core)
// =============================================================================

// Protocol Buffers wire-format codec: varint, tag, wire-types, ZigZag,
// fixed32/64, float, string, bytes, bool. Low-level surface intended
// for gRPC/Connect clients. Higher-level schema-driven codegen is a
// meta-programming follow-up.
public module protobuf;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.95: Tracing (Depends on: core, text, collections, async, sync, time)
// =============================================================================

// Distributed tracing (OpenTelemetry-compatible): TraceId/SpanId,
// SpanContext, Span, Tracer, Sampler, SpanProcessor, SpanExporter,
// W3C Trace Context (traceparent/tracestate) propagation.
// Spec: docs/detailed/tracing-architecture.md
public module tracing;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 4.96: Metrics (Depends on: core, text, collections, sync, time)
// =============================================================================

// Prometheus-compatible metrics: Counter, UpDownCounter, Gauge,
// Histogram with bucketed observations, label-cardinality control,
// Registry, and text-format exposition.
// Spec: docs/detailed/metrics-architecture.md
public module metrics;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 5: Network (Depends on: core, text, collections, io, async)
// =============================================================================

// Networking: TCP, UDP, HTTP, WebSocket, TLS.
public module net;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 5: Diagnostics (Depends on: mem.diagnostics, async.diagnostics)
// =============================================================================

// User-facing observability umbrella — composes layer-local CBGR
// (mem.diagnostics) and async runtime (async.diagnostics) snapshot
// surfaces into a single import target. Future folds: tracing/metrics
// observability handle, capability audit log, symbolicated panic stack,
// live_allocations iterator. Tracked in #197.
// Spec: docs/architecture/observability.md (TODO)
public module diagnostics;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 5: Shell scripting (Depends on: io.process, async.{cancellation,
// nursery, channel, generator, backoff}, time, text, base.env)
// =============================================================================

// Verum Shell Scripting Framework — sh#"..." executor, type-safe escape,
// streaming, structured concurrency, typed command DSLs, CBGR resources.
// Spec: shell-scripting spec
public module shell;

// Verum CLI Framework — declarative argv parsing, refinement-typed args,
// adaptive help, completion / manpage emitters, typed dispatch.
// Spec: cli-framework spec
public module cli;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 5.5: Service mesh (Depends on: core.net.http2, core.protobuf, core.net.tls)
// =============================================================================

// Service-mesh integration — Envoy xDS v3 (ADS) client plus future
// Istio / Consul / Linkerd / OSM backends.
public module mesh;

// =============================================================================
// Layer 6: Cognitive (Depends on: all previous + MLIR GPU backend)
// =============================================================================

// Cognitive computing: Tensors, GPU, autodiff, neural networks.
// Deferred: GPU/tensor compute requires hardware-specific backends.
// See core/math/ for CPU-based tensor, linear algebra, and ML operations.
// public module cognitive;

// =============================================================================
// Layer A: Application (Depends on: math, io, collections)
// =============================================================================

// Action layer (Diakrisis Actic) — DC-side stdlib mirror of
// `core.math` (OC-side). Exposes the eight ε-primitives, articulation
// / enactment algebra, the Theorem 17.T1 effect catalogue with
// concrete monad implementations (Pure, Reader, Writer, State,
// Probability, Quantum, Exception, Nondeterm, Io), Ludics
// designs (Theorem 120.T), and gauge-coherence audit helpers
// (`verify_epsilon`, `gauge_consistency`, streaming
// `CoherenceMonitor`).
//

// Pre-fix `core.action` was reachable only via direct file-path
// mounting (`mount core.action.articulation`) — it had NO
// declaration in `core/mod.vr`, so it was missing from the
// stdlib root surface. Downstream modules that want
// `core.action.*` could mount sub-paths but couldn't reach the
// umbrella, and `core.prelude.*` couldn't re-export anything from
// it. Wiring it as a peer module of `theory_interop` (the OC-DC
// duality bridge sits under theory_interop/bridges) restores the
// symmetric module-tree shape and lets the prelude evolve to
// re-export common Actic helpers in a follow-up.
public module action;

// Embedded scripting — Verum-as-a-scripting-language for host applications.
// A host program compiles and runs Verum scripts at runtime, in-process, on
// the same VBC interpreter it runs on (the capability a game engine gets from
// embedding Lua). Backed by core.intrinsics.runtime.scripting.
public module script;

// =============================================================================
// Layer A: Verification + Logic Foundations (Depends on: math, base, smt)
// =============================================================================
//

// Eight modules that sit alongside `core.action` as the
// verification + logic foundation layer. Pre-fix every one of these
// was unrooted in `core/mod.vr` despite shipping production code
// + downstream consumers — discovered via a side-by-side compare
// of the directory listing against the registered-modules grep.
// Each gets a real module declaration here AND (for the six that
// previously lacked one) a freshly-authored `mod.vr` re-exporting
// the children with a doc-header tying the module into the
// architectural layering.
//

//  * `core.proof` — verification kernel surface
//  (contracts_old, kernel_bridge, pcc,
//  reflection, tactics)
//  * `core.types` — research-grade type machinery
//  (poly_kinds, qtt, two_level)
//  * `core.verify` — first-class verification API
//  (level, attempt, certificate, coherence)
//  * `core.logic` — non-classical logic kernels
//  (kripke, linear)
//  * `core.concurrency` — typed concurrency models
//  (process, session)
//  * `core.eval` — evaluation strategies
//  (cbpv)
//  * `core.control` — control-flow primitives
//  (continuation)
//  * `core.database` — Spindle DB client framework
//  (common, sqlite, postgres, mysql)
public module proof;
public module types;

// The `@arch_module(...)` vocabulary itself — `Foundation`,
// `MsfsStratum` and `Lifecycle` (declared in `core.types` and re-
// exported here), the canonical-field roster, and the attribute's
// parser + error type.  Every attested module in the library names
// these three, so the module that DEFINES them belongs on the root
// surface; like `core.money` above it was reachable only by exact
// path.  Sits next to `types` because that is where its vocabulary
// comes from.
public module architecture;
public module verify;
public module logic;
public module concurrency;
public module eval;
public module control;
public module database;

// Theory interoperation: a computational ∞-topos of formally
// represented theories. Theory loading (Yoneda), translation
// (Kan extensions), coherence (descent), auditing, and a
// JSON-RPC wire protocol for external tooling.
// Built on: math.epistemic, math.infinity_topos,
// math.kan_extension, math.quantum_logic, math.giry,
// math.cohesive, math.day_convolution.
public module theory_interop;

// =============================================================================
// Layer M: Meta (Compile-time, no runtime dependencies)
// =============================================================================

// Meta-programming: Code generation, type reflection, quasi-quotation.
// Operates entirely at compile-time with zero runtime overhead.
// Provides: TokenStream, TypeInfo, BuildAssets, quote { }, @derive.
// Spec: docs/architecture/meta-audit.md
public module meta;

// =============================================================================
// Prelude - commonly used items automatically available
// =============================================================================

// The prelude is automatically imported in every Verum module.
// It contains the most fundamental types && functions.
public module prelude {
    // =========================================================================
    // From base — CURATED, never a directory glob
    // =========================================================================
    //
    // INCLUSION LAW: a name belongs in the prelude only if the LANGUAGE
    // ITSELF requires it to be in scope everywhere — the types the
    // grammar and the checker name (`Maybe`, `Result`, `Ordering`), the
    // protocols `@derive(...)` and generic bounds refer to, the
    // iteration model, the panic/assert built-in family, the semantic
    // memory types, and the coercion markers the unifier consults.
    // EVERYTHING ELSE IS MOUNTED EXPLICITLY at its use site.
    //
    // Why this is not a glob: `public mount super.base.*;` published all
    // 316 top-level names of base/ — including domain utilities — into
    // every program, so five different modules each contributed a free
    // `parse`, two a `matches`, two a `generate`, plus ambient `and`,
    // `or`, `format`, `cmp`, `nil`, `v4`, `compile`, `retry`.  A bare
    // `parse("1.2.3")` in a file with no mounts silently bound to the
    // META-TERM parser and returned `Ok(MeTerm(1.2.3))`; `generate()`
    // reported `Time.now` vs `Ulid.now` as competing candidates.  That
    // is precisely what design principle #2 («No Magic: all dependencies
    // explicit via mount») forbids, and it is the ambient-collision
    // class that forces the checker into last-write-wins guesswork.
    public mount super.base.maybe.*;
    public mount super.base.result.*;
    public mount super.base.ordering.*;
    public mount super.base.protocols.*;
    public mount super.base.ops.*;
    public mount super.base.error.*;
    // ITERATOR — the model, not its 47 adapter types.
    //
    // `super.base.iterator.*` published `MappedIter`, `FilterIter`,
    // `ZipIter`, `WindowsIter` and 43 more into every program. None of
    // them is a name the LANGUAGE requires in scope: they are what
    // `.map(…)` and `.filter(…)` RETURN, and a program that never
    // annotates one never says them. The inclusion law above asks for
    // the model — the protocols the checker names, the range types the
    // grammar builds for `a..b`, and the constructors that start an
    // iteration.
    public mount super.base.iterator.{
        Iterator, DoubleEndedIterator, ExactSizeIterator,
        FusedIterator, IntoIterator,
        Range, RangeInclusive,
        range, range_inclusive, count_from, repeat, repeat_n, once,
        empty, repeat_with, once_with, from_fn, unfold, successors,
        unzip,
    };
    public mount super.base.panic.*;
    public mount super.base.memory.*;
    public mount super.base.cell.*;
    public mount super.base.primitives.*;
    public mount super.base.coercion.*;

    // From text.format — the LANGUAGE inserts these names: `f"{x:?}"`
    // desugars to a bare `format_debug(&x)` call (see
    // `verum_fast_parser/src/safe_interpolation.rs::format_debug_call`),
    // and the TEXT-DEBUG-STATIC-1 codegen rewrite substitutes
    // `format_debug_text` for the concrete-Text receiver IN PLACE, so
    // it must resolve exactly where `format_debug` does.  Under the
    // inclusion law these are the `panic`/`assert` family's kin — a
    // name the grammar itself emits must be in scope everywhere.
    // (`f"{x}"` display needs no name: the interpolation machinery
    // reaches the formatter without one — measured with the prelude
    // off.  Only the Debug arm rides a bare call.)
    public mount super.text.format.{format_debug, format_debug_text};

    // From collections (common types)
    // The DEFINING modules, not the `collections` umbrella.
    //
    // rings.toml states the rule and the reason: "ring 0 cannot use the
    // convenience of `mount collections.List`, because naming the umbrella
    // makes the edge point at ring 2".  `collections.list`, `.map` and
    // `.set` ARE ring 0 — they are the language's vocabulary, which is why
    // they were placed there — while `collections` as a whole (btree,
    // deque, lru, bloom) is ring 2.  The prelude was committing the very
    // mistake that file documents, and every `mount core.*;` in the tree
    // inherited it: 33 upward edges that named a dependency nobody had.
    //
    // `collections/mod.vr` only re-exports these three (`public mount
    // .list.List;`), so the binding is identical — this removes a hop that
    // hid which module the type comes from, and nothing else.
    // `List` / `Map` / `Set` — the DEFINING modules, which are ring 0.
    //
    // These were briefly removed, on an experiment that turned out to
    // answer the wrong question.  With `@no_implicit_prelude` the form
    // `let l: List<Int> = List.new(); l.len()` compiles and runs, so the
    // compiler clearly knows the type without this line — but that tests
    // whether `List` can be USED without the prelude, not whether it can
    // be MOUNTED FROM it.  Fifteen files in core/ write
    // `mount core.prelude.{… List …}`, and removal broke every one of
    // them with `E401: cannot find List in module core.prelude`.
    //
    // `core.prelude` is a PUBLISHED SURFACE, not an internal convenience:
    // what it exports is what a `mount core.prelude.{X}` may name, here
    // and in user code.  A name reachable by inference is not the same as
    // a name reachable by import, and only the second is a contract.
    //
    // Named through `collections.list` / `.map` / `.set` rather than the
    // `collections` umbrella, per rings.toml: those three submodules ARE
    // ring 0 — they are the language's vocabulary — while `collections` as
    // a whole is ring 2, and naming the umbrella would point the edge
    // there.
    public mount super.collections.list.List;
    public mount super.collections.map.Map;
    public mount super.collections.set.Set;

    // From io (common functions)
    // `print` / `println` / `eprint` / `eprintln` are NOT re-exported.
    //
    // MEASURED, not assumed: with `@no_implicit_prelude` — the prelude
    // switched off entirely — `print("...")` still compiles and runs.  The
    // compiler provides it as a built-in form, exactly as CLAUDE.md's
    // "no `!` anywhere" table says.  These four lines published names the
    // language already had, and charged 33 upward ring edges plus two of
    // the three prelude cycles for it.
    // `io.File` and `io.read_to_string` are NOT re-exported: filesystem
    // API, not language.  ONE file in core/ used them ambiently.

    // (The older text.format note that stood here said "NOT
    // re-exported" while also observing that surfacing the names makes
    // Debug dispatch work — the contradiction shipped as a live
    // `unbound variable: format_debug` on every `f"{x:?}"`.  The
    // Debug-arm pair is re-exported ABOVE, with the inclusion-law
    // rationale.)

    // From sync (common primitives)
    // sync is NOT re-exported: concurrency is a library.  Fourteen files
    // used `Mutex` / `RwLock` / `AtomicInt` / `AtomicBool` ambiently and
    // now mount them — nine of those are inside sync/ itself and mount
    // `sync.mutex` / `sync.atomic` directly, which is also the ring-0
    // discipline: name the DEFINING module, not the umbrella.

    // From intrinsics.runtime.tier — execution-tier introspection so
    // `if is_interpreted() { ... }` / `get_tier()` work without an explicit
    // mount (TIER-DETECT-PRELUDE-1, T0206).
    // `intrinsics.runtime.tier` is NOT re-exported: a runtime query, not
    // language.  ZERO files in core/ used it ambiently.

    // From time (common types)
    // time is NOT re-exported: a library.  Five files used `Duration` /
    // `Instant` / `Time` ambiently and now mount them.

    // From math (common functions and types)
    // math is NOT re-exported here.
    //
    // §4ter: a name belongs to the prelude iff the LANGUAGE requires it.
    // The grammar names `Maybe`, `Result`, `Ordering`; it does not name
    // `sqrt`, `Matrix`, or Euler's number.  Mathematics is a library.
    //
    // This block published 28 names, and the measured damage is on record:
    // fourteen of the prelude's twenty math functions were being served by
    // sqlite's free functions instead of math's, because the binding was
    // by bare (name, arity) and sqlite got there first (T0220, T0670).
    // A name that is ambient everywhere is a name that anyone can win.
    //
    // `E` deserves its own sentence.  A one-letter constant published into
    // every file collides with the universal spelling of the error type
    // parameter: measured across core/, `E` reads as a TYPE PARAMETER in
    // 16 files and as Euler's number in 2 — including inside
    // `core/base/maybe.vr`, where `Result<T, E>` is the subject.
    //
    // MEASURED cost of removal: EIGHT files, seven of them inside math/
    // itself, which now mount `math.elementary` / `math.constants`
    // directly — a sibling import that says where the function comes
    // from, instead of an ambient one that does not.

    // context.standard is NOT re-exported here.
    //
    // It used to be, with this justification: "intentionally surfaced at
    // the prelude level so request-handling code does not have to repeat
    // `mount context.standard.{...}` in every middleware / handler file."
    // That is a convenience argument, and §4ter of
    // docs/architecture/core-reference-architecture.md answers it: a name
    // belongs to the prelude IF AND ONLY IF THE LANGUAGE REQUIRES IT.
    // `Logger`, `Database`, `Auth`, `Config`, `Cache`, `Metrics`,
    // `Tracer`, `Clock`, `Random`, `FileSystem` are services, not
    // language.  Principle 2 — no magic, every dependency explicit via
    // `mount` — forbids it a second time.
    //
    // The twelve names were also among the most collision-prone in the
    // tree: `Database` alone is declared three times in core/
    // (`context.standard`, `database/common/db_handle`,
    // `database/sqlite/native/l7_api/database`), and every file received
    // all three whether it asked or not.
    //
    // MEASURED before removal: exactly SIX files in core/ bound one of the
    // twelve ambiently — three `Logger`, three `Random` — and each now
    // mounts it explicitly.  The other ten names had no ambient consumer
    // at all.  (The first count was 106 files; refining it to real binding
    // positions, then excluding files that already mount the name from
    // their own module, took it to 6.  Each refinement removed a category
    // of false positive, not a judgement call.)
}

// =============================================================================
// Version Information
// =============================================================================

// Standard library version
public const VERSION: &Text = "0.1.0";

// Standard library version tuple
public const VERSION_INFO: (Int, Int, Int) = (0, 1, 0);
